//! Schema migrations and a fluent DDL builder for the `data` package.
//!
//! This module gives a Kyte application a small, explicit way to evolve a
//! database schema over time. A migration is an ordered pair of SQL scripts: an
//! `up` that moves the schema forward (create a table, add a column) and a
//! `down` that undoes exactly that step. The set of migrations that have already
//! run is remembered in a dedicated tracking table inside the SAME database, so
//! the record of "how far this database has been migrated" travels with the data
//! itself rather than living in a separate file or in application memory.
//!
//! ## How the version bookkeeping works
//!
//! Every migration carries a `version`, a caller-chosen monotonic number
//! (conventionally a timestamp or a simple counter). The tracking table
//! [`TRACKING_TABLE`] stores one row per applied migration, keyed by that
//! version. [`currentVersion`] reads the highest recorded version, and that
//! single number is the whole state of the world: [`migrate`] applies every
//! migration whose version is strictly greater than it, and [`rollbackTo`]
//! reverses every migration whose version is above a target. There is no
//! checksum and no name comparison, so versions must be assigned once and never
//! renumbered, and a version must map to the same `up`/`down` for the life of
//! the database.
//!
//! ## Ordering and direction
//!
//! Both runners sort the supplied migrations by version first ([`sortByVersion`])
//! so the caller may pass them in any order. [`migrate`] walks the sorted list
//! low to high and applies pending steps forward; [`rollbackTo`] walks it high to
//! low and applies `down` scripts in reverse, which is the only correct order
//! when later steps depend on earlier ones. Each applied or reversed step is
//! recorded or forgotten in the tracking table in the same transaction-less
//! sequence of `exec` calls the migration issues.
//!
//! ## The DDL builder
//!
//! Writing `CREATE TABLE` SQL by hand is where migration scripts pick up typos
//! and dialect drift, so [`Table`] offers a chainable builder that assembles a
//! column and constraint list and renders it to a single statement. It is a
//! convenience for authoring the `up` string of a migration; it does not run
//! anything itself.
//!
//! The SQL emitted here is written to be portable across the drivers the `data`
//! package speaks to (`BIGINT`, `TEXT`, standard `PRIMARY KEY`/`FOREIGN KEY`/
//! `UNIQUE` clauses) and uses positional `$1`, `$2` placeholders for the
//! tracking-table writes, matching the binding convention of the [`db`] interface.

import string;
import list;
import data.db;

/// One reversible schema change: a version, a human label, and its two SQL
/// scripts.
///
/// A `Migration` is a plain value record. `up` is the SQL that moves the schema
/// forward and `down` is the SQL that exactly undoes it; the framework never
/// generates one from the other, so a wrong or empty `down` will silently fail
/// to reverse the step. `version` is the ordering and identity key used
/// throughout this module (see [`currentVersion`], [`migrate`], [`rollbackTo`]);
/// `name` is stored only for readability in the tracking table.
pub struct Migration {
    /// Monotonic ordering and identity key. Steps run in ascending order and a
    /// version is applied only if it exceeds the recorded [`currentVersion`].
    pub version: long,
    /// Human-readable label, stored in the tracking table for diagnostics. It
    /// plays no part in ordering or in deciding whether a step has run.
    pub name: string,
    /// SQL that moves the schema forward, executed by [`migrate`].
    pub up: string,
    /// SQL that reverses this exact step, executed by [`rollbackTo`]. The author
    /// is responsible for making it the true inverse of `up`.
    pub down: string,
    /// Builds a migration from its four fields verbatim.
    init(version: long, name: string, up: string, down: string) {
        self.version = version; self.name = name; self.up = up; self.down = down;
    }
}
/// Convenience constructor for a [`Migration`], for callers who prefer a
/// free function over the initialiser.
pub fn migration(version: long, name: string, up: string, down: string): Migration {
    return Migration(version, name, up, down);
}

/// The outcome of a [`run`] (or [`applyPending`]) call: how many steps were
/// applied this time and, if a step failed, why.
///
/// `applied` counts the migrations whose `up` and ledger write both committed on
/// this call, so a clean re-run of an up-to-date database yields `applied == 0`
/// and an empty `err`. `err` is empty on success and otherwise carries the
/// failing migration's version, name, and the driver's message. This is the
/// value a `--migrate` app mode inspects to choose its process exit code, which
/// is in turn what the orchestrator gates the rollout on.
pub struct MigrateReport {
    /// Number of migrations applied on this call (0 when already up to date).
    pub applied: int,
    /// Empty on success; the failure reason otherwise.
    pub err: string,
    init(applied: int, err: string) { self.applied = applied; self.err = err; }
    /// True when the run applied every pending step without error.
    pub fn ok(self: MigrateReport): bool { return self.err.length == 0; }
}

/// Name of the single-row table used to serialise concurrent migration runners.
///
/// See [`tryLock`]. It lives in the application's own database alongside
/// [`TRACKING_TABLE`]. A crashed migrator can leave its row behind (there is no
/// automatic lease expiry); clearing it is a manual `DELETE FROM
/// schema_migrations_lock` or a call to [`unlock`].
pub const LOCK_TABLE: string = "schema_migrations_lock";
/// SQL that creates the lock table if absent. The `id` primary key is what makes
/// a second concurrent [`tryLock`] insert fail, which is the whole mechanism.
fn createLockTableSql(): string { return "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS " + LOCK_TABLE + " (id INT PRIMARY KEY)"; }

/// Attempts to claim the exclusive migration lock; returns whether this caller
/// now holds it.
///
/// The lock is a single fixed row (`id = 1`) in [`LOCK_TABLE`]. Claiming it is a
/// plain `INSERT`, and the table's PRIMARY KEY means that when two runners race,
/// exactly one insert succeeds and the other comes back as a unique-key
/// violation. That makes the mechanism portable across every driver the `data`
/// package speaks to, with no dialect-specific advisory-lock call
/// (`pg_advisory_lock`, `GET_LOCK`, `sp_getapplock`) required. A non-violation
/// error (for example a lost connection) is also reported as "not acquired" so
/// the caller never proceeds to migrate on a broken connection. Release with
/// [`unlock`].
pub async fn tryLock(conn: db.Connection): bool {
    let c = await conn.exec(createLockTableSql(), list.List<db.DbValue>());
    let claim = list.List<db.DbValue>();
    claim.push(db.dbLong(1));
    let r = await conn.exec("INSERT INTO " + LOCK_TABLE + " (id) VALUES ($1)", claim);
    return r.ok();
}

/// Releases the migration lock claimed by [`tryLock`] by deleting its row. Safe
/// to call when the lock is not held (the delete simply affects no rows).
pub async fn unlock(conn: db.Connection): void {
    let r = await conn.exec("DELETE FROM " + LOCK_TABLE + " WHERE id = 1", list.List<db.DbValue>());
}

/// Name of the table that records which migrations have been applied.
///
/// It lives inside the application's own database. Every SQL statement in this
/// module that touches migration state ([`createTrackingTableSql`],
/// [`recordSql`], [`forgetSql`], [`currentVersion`]) references this one name,
/// so changing it would orphan an existing history.
pub const TRACKING_TABLE: string = "schema_migrations";

/// SQL that creates the tracking table if it is not already present.
///
/// [`migrate`] runs this first so a brand-new database can be migrated without a
/// separate setup step. `IF NOT EXISTS` makes it safe to run on every migrate.
/// The schema is deliberately minimal: `version` as the `BIGINT` primary key and
/// a non-null `name`.
pub fn createTrackingTableSql(): string {
    return "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS " + TRACKING_TABLE + " (version BIGINT PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL)";
}
/// SQL that records an applied migration, with `$1`, `$2` bound to version and
/// name. Used by [`migrate`] immediately after a successful `up`.
fn recordSql(): string { return "INSERT INTO " + TRACKING_TABLE + " (version, name) VALUES ($1, $2)"; }
/// SQL that removes a migration's record, with `$1` bound to its version. Used by
/// [`rollbackTo`] after a successful `down` so the step counts as unapplied.
fn forgetSql(): string { return "DELETE FROM " + TRACKING_TABLE + " WHERE version = $1"; }

/// Returns a new list holding the migrations sorted by ascending version.
///
/// This lets callers pass migrations in any order while both runners still see a
/// deterministic sequence. It copies the input into a fresh list and does an
/// in-place selection sort on the copy, so the caller's list is left untouched.
/// Selection sort is chosen for simplicity: migration sets are small, so its
/// quadratic cost never matters. The comparison is on `version` alone, so two
/// migrations sharing a version keep their relative input order arbitrarily,
/// which is why versions are expected to be unique.
fn sortByVersion(ms: list.List<Migration>): list.List<Migration> {
    let out = list.List<Migration>();
    let i = 0;
    while (i < ms.size()) { out.push(ms.at(i)); i = i + 1; }
    let a = 0;
    while (a < out.size()) {
        let min = a;
        let b = a + 1;
        while (b < out.size()) {
            if (out.at(b).version < out.at(min).version) { min = b; }
            b = b + 1;
        }
        if (min != a) {
            let tmp = out.at(a);
            out.set(a, out.at(min));
            out.set(min, tmp);
        }
        a = a + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

/// Reads the highest applied migration version from the tracking table.
///
/// Returns `0` when no migration has been recorded, which is also the sentinel
/// for a database whose tracking table does not yet exist or holds no rows. Note
/// that `0` therefore doubles as "nothing applied", so a real migration must use
/// a version greater than `0` to be treated as pending by [`migrate`]. The
/// `?? db.Row()` guard defends against a missing first row: if the result set
/// reports a row but `get(0)` yields nothing, an empty [`db.Row`] is used and its
/// first column read back as a long.
pub async fn currentVersion(conn: db.Connection): long {
    let rs = await conn.query("SELECT version FROM " + TRACKING_TABLE + " ORDER BY version DESC LIMIT 1", list.List<db.DbValue>());
    if (rs.rows.size() == 0) { return 0; }
    let row = rs.rows.get(0) ?? db.Row();
    return row.get(0).asLong();
}

/// Applies every pending migration in order, each step in its own transaction,
/// and reports the outcome.
///
/// The flow is: ensure the tracking table exists, read the current version, sort
/// the supplied migrations, then walk them low to high applying each one whose
/// `version` exceeds the recorded version. Applying a step runs `begin`, then the
/// migration's `up`, then the `(version, name)` ledger insert via [`recordSql`],
/// then `commit`, so the schema change and its bookkeeping land together: a crash
/// or error between them rolls back and the step is neither applied nor recorded.
/// On the first failing step it rolls that step back, stops, and returns a
/// [`MigrateReport`] naming the failure; earlier committed steps stay applied.
///
/// A caveat remains on engines whose DDL is not transactional (for example MySQL
/// auto-commits `CREATE TABLE`): there `rollback` cannot undo the `up`, so a
/// failure between `up` and the ledger insert can leave that one step applied but
/// unrecorded. On engines with transactional DDL (PostgreSQL, and the ledger
/// write on all of them) each step is atomic.
///
/// This does NOT take the [`tryLock`] guard: it assumes the caller has already
/// serialised runners (the orchestrator runs it once) or does not need to. Use
/// [`run`] for the guarded, single-call entry point.
pub async fn applyPending(conn: db.Connection, migrations: list.List<Migration>): MigrateReport {
    let e0 = await conn.exec(createTrackingTableSql(), list.List<db.DbValue>());
    let applied = await currentVersion(conn);
    let ordered = sortByVersion(migrations);
    let n = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while (i < ordered.size()) {
        let m = ordered.at(i);
        if (m.version > applied) {
            let b = await conn.begin();
            if (b.hasError()) { return MigrateReport(n, `migration ${m.version} (${m.name}): begin failed: ${b.err.message}`); }
            let up = await conn.exec(m.up, list.List<db.DbValue>());
            if (up.hasError()) {
                let rb = await conn.rollback();
                return MigrateReport(n, `migration ${m.version} (${m.name}) failed: ${up.err.message}`);
            }
            let rec = list.List<db.DbValue>();
            rec.push(db.dbLong(m.version));
            rec.push(db.dbText(m.name));
            let r = await conn.exec(recordSql(), rec);
            if (r.hasError()) {
                let rb = await conn.rollback();
                return MigrateReport(n, `migration ${m.version} (${m.name}) recorded failed: ${r.err.message}`);
            }
            let cm = await conn.commit();
            if (cm.hasError()) { return MigrateReport(n, `migration ${m.version} (${m.name}) commit failed: ${cm.err.message}`); }
            n = n + 1;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return MigrateReport(n, "");
}

/// Applies every pending migration in order and returns how many ran.
///
/// A thin back-compatible wrapper over [`applyPending`] that returns only the
/// applied count (discarding any error detail), so calling it twice in a row
/// yields the pending count and then `0`. New callers that need the failure
/// reason or the run-once guard should prefer [`run`].
pub async fn migrate(conn: db.Connection, migrations: list.List<Migration>): int {
    let rep = await applyPending(conn, migrations);
    return rep.applied;
}

/// The guarded, single-call entry point: claim the run-once lock, apply all
/// pending migrations, release the lock, and report.
///
/// This is what a `--migrate` app mode calls. It takes [`tryLock`] so that when
/// several processes start together (N app replicas self-migrating on boot)
/// exactly one proceeds; the losers return immediately with a non-empty `err`
/// noting the lock is held, which the caller surfaces as a non-zero exit. In the
/// orchestrator's gated model the lock is always free because the migrate step
/// runs once, on its own, before any replica starts. The lock is released
/// whether the apply succeeded or failed, so a later retry can proceed; only a
/// hard crash mid-run leaves the lock row behind (clear it with [`unlock`]).
pub async fn run(conn: db.Connection, migrations: list.List<Migration>): MigrateReport {
    let got = await tryLock(conn);
    if (!got) { return MigrateReport(0, "migration lock is held by another process"); }
    let rep = await applyPending(conn, migrations);
    await unlock(conn);
    return rep;
}

/// Read-only: how many migrations are pending (would be applied by [`run`]).
///
/// Ensures the tracking table exists, reads the current version, and counts the
/// supplied migrations whose `version` exceeds it. Applies nothing, so it is the
/// query a readiness check or a `--migrate --status` flag uses to report drift
/// without touching the schema.
pub async fn pending(conn: db.Connection, migrations: list.List<Migration>): int {
    let e0 = await conn.exec(createTrackingTableSql(), list.List<db.DbValue>());
    let applied = await currentVersion(conn);
    let n = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while (i < migrations.size()) {
        if (migrations.at(i).version > applied) { n = n + 1; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return n;
}

/// Reverses every applied migration above `target` and returns how many ran.
///
/// It sorts the migrations, reads the current version, then walks them high to
/// low, and for each step whose version is both greater than `target` and less
/// than or equal to the applied version, it runs the `down` script and then
/// deletes that step's tracking row via [`forgetSql`]. Reversing top-down is the
/// only safe order when later steps were built on earlier ones. Passing
/// `target = 0` rolls the schema all the way back to empty.
///
/// The `version <= applied` guard means only migrations that were actually
/// applied are reversed, so passing steps that were never run is harmless. As
/// with [`migrate`], there is no enclosing transaction, so a failing `down`
/// leaves the schema partway reverted.
pub async fn rollbackTo(conn: db.Connection, migrations: list.List<Migration>, target: long): int {
    let ordered = sortByVersion(migrations);
    let applied = await currentVersion(conn);
    let n = 0;
    let i = ordered.size() - 1;
    while (i >= 0) {
        let m = ordered.at(i);
        if (m.version > target && m.version <= applied) {
            let dn = await conn.exec(m.down, list.List<db.DbValue>());
            let rec = list.List<db.DbValue>();
            rec.push(db.dbLong(m.version));
            let r = await conn.exec(forgetSql(), rec);
            n = n + 1;
        }
        i = i - 1;
    }
    return n;
}


/// A fluent builder for a single `CREATE TABLE` statement.
///
/// `Table` accumulates two lists, one of column definitions and one of
/// table-level constraints, and renders them into one statement with [`toSql`] or
/// [`toSqlIfNotExists`]. It is a `class` (reference type) so that each chained
/// method can mutate the same instance and return `self`, letting calls read as a
/// pipeline: `table("users").id().column(...).unique(...)`. The builder only
/// produces a string; it never talks to a database. Feed its output into a
/// [`Migration`]'s `up`.
pub class Table {
    /// Table name, interpolated directly into the emitted DDL.
    pub name: string,
    /// Column definitions in insertion order, each a ready-made SQL fragment
    /// such as `"email TEXT NOT NULL"`. Rendered before the constraints.
    pub cols: list.List<string>,
    /// Table-level constraint clauses (primary key, foreign key, unique),
    /// appended after all columns in the emitted statement.
    pub constraints: list.List<string>,
    /// Starts an empty builder for the named table with no columns or
    /// constraints.
    init(name: string) {
        self.name = name;
        self.cols = list.List<string>();
        self.constraints = list.List<string>();
    }
    /// Adds a column and returns `self` for chaining.
    ///
    /// Builds the fragment as `name sqlType`, and appends `opts` when it is
    /// non-empty, so callers pass modifiers like `"NOT NULL"` or `"DEFAULT 0"`
    /// through `opts`. The strings are used verbatim; no quoting or validation is
    /// applied, so the caller controls the exact SQL.
    pub fn column(self: Table, name: string, sqlType: string, opts: string): Table {
        let c = name + " " + sqlType;
        if (opts.length > 0) { c = c + " " + opts; }
        self.cols.push(c);
        return self;
    }
    /// Adds the conventional surrogate primary key column `id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY`
    /// and returns `self`. A shorthand for the near-universal auto-id column.
    pub fn id(self: Table): Table { self.cols.push("id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY"); return self; }
    /// Adds a table-level `PRIMARY KEY (...)` constraint over `cols` (a raw
    /// comma-separated column list) and returns `self`. Use this for composite
    /// keys; for a single surrogate key prefer [`Table.id`].
    pub fn primaryKey(self: Table, cols: string): Table { self.constraints.push("PRIMARY KEY (" + cols + ")"); return self; }
    /// Adds a `FOREIGN KEY (col) REFERENCES refTable (refCol)` constraint and
    /// returns `self`. All three names are inserted verbatim.
    pub fn foreignKey(self: Table, col: string, refTable: string, refCol: string): Table {
        self.constraints.push("FOREIGN KEY (" + col + ") REFERENCES " + refTable + " (" + refCol + ")");
        return self;
    }
    /// Adds a `UNIQUE (...)` constraint over `cols` (a raw comma-separated column
    /// list) and returns `self`.
    pub fn unique(self: Table, cols: string): Table { self.constraints.push("UNIQUE (" + cols + ")"); return self; }

    /// Renders the inside of the parentheses: all columns, then all constraints,
    /// joined with commas.
    ///
    /// Columns come first, comma-separated, then each constraint is appended with
    /// a leading `", "`. Shared by [`toSql`] and [`toSqlIfNotExists`] so the two
    /// differ only in their `CREATE TABLE` prefix.
    fn body(self: Table): string {
        let parts = "";
        let i = 0;
        while (i < self.cols.size()) {
            if (i > 0) { parts = parts + ", "; }
            parts = parts + self.cols.at(i);
            i = i + 1;
        }
        let j = 0;
        while (j < self.constraints.size()) {
            parts = parts + ", " + self.constraints.at(j);
            j = j + 1;
        }
        return parts;
    }
    /// Renders the full `CREATE TABLE name (...)` statement.
    pub fn toSql(self: Table): string { return "CREATE TABLE " + self.name + " (" + self.body() + ")"; }
    /// Renders the statement with `IF NOT EXISTS`, so it is a no-op when the
    /// table already exists. Handy for a migration meant to be safe to re-run.
    pub fn toSqlIfNotExists(self: Table): string { return "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS " + self.name + " (" + self.body() + ")"; }
}
/// Convenience constructor for a [`Table`] builder, the usual entry point to the
/// fluent chain.
pub fn table(name: string): Table { return Table(name); }
/// Returns `DROP TABLE IF EXISTS name`, the natural `down` counterpart to a
/// `CREATE TABLE` built with [`Table.toSql`]. `IF NOT EXISTS` makes it safe when
/// the table is already gone.
pub fn dropTableSql(name: string): string { return "DROP TABLE IF EXISTS " + name; }

/// Flags a migration whose `up` is NOT expand-only, returning a warning string
/// (empty when the migration is safe to apply during a rolling deploy).
///
/// During a rolling update both the old and the new version of an app run
/// against the same schema for a window. A migration is safe in that window only
/// if it is *additive*: creating a table, adding a nullable column, adding an
/// index. Destructive or tightening changes (`DROP`, `RENAME`, `ALTER ... TYPE`,
/// `SET NOT NULL`) break the still-running old version and must ship as a
/// *separate, later* release, after every replica already speaks the new schema.
/// This is a lexical guard, not a parser: it upper-cases the `up` SQL and looks
/// for the risky fragments, so it can over-warn on a keyword inside a string
/// literal. It is meant to surface a review question, not to block, so callers
/// (a `--migrate` mode, a CI check) typically print it as a warning.
pub fn lintExpandOnly(m: Migration): string {
    let up = string.toUpperCase(m.up);
    let hits = "";
    if (string.contains(up, "DROP TABLE") || string.contains(up, "DROP COLUMN")) { hits = hits + " DROP"; }
    if (string.contains(up, "RENAME")) { hits = hits + " RENAME"; }
    if (string.contains(up, "ALTER COLUMN") && string.contains(up, "TYPE")) { hits = hits + " ALTER-TYPE"; }
    if (string.contains(up, "SET NOT NULL")) { hits = hits + " SET-NOT-NULL"; }
    if (hits.length == 0) { return ""; }
    return `migration ${m.version} (${m.name}) is not expand-only (${hits} ): apply it as a separate release AFTER all replicas run the new code, or the still-running old version may break during the rollout`;
}

/// Runs [`lintExpandOnly`] over a whole migration list and returns one warning
/// per non-expand-only step (empty list when every step is additive). Handy for a
/// pre-deploy CI check that wants to report all offenders at once.
pub fn lintAll(migrations: list.List<Migration>): list.List<string> {
    let out = list.List<string>();
    let i = 0;
    while (i < migrations.size()) {
        let w = lintExpandOnly(migrations.at(i));
        if (w.length > 0) { out.push(w); }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}
